---
import PageLayout from '../layouts/PageLayout.astro';
import DefinitionBox from '../components/content/DefinitionBox.astro';
import Callout from '../components/content/Callout.astro';
import CompareTable from '../components/content/CompareTable.astro';
import FundamentalTriad from '../components/diagrams/FundamentalTriad.astro';

const principles = [
  {
    n: '01',
    tone: 'primary',
    title: 'Two audiences, one platform, one accountable human.',
    body: 'Agents act. A developer approves the work and answers for the result. Serve both, and remember who carries the consequences.',
  },
  {
    n: '02',
    tone: 'amber',
    title: 'Agents choose before they build.',
    body: 'Measure what agents choose when nobody names a platform. The working hypothesis is that public documentation, code, and discussion influence that choice.',
  },
  {
    n: '03',
    tone: 'purple',
    title: 'Failures are silent until instrumented.',
    body: 'A developer whose AI tool produces a broken integration rarely files a ticket. Evaluations, agent traffic analytics, and a reporting channel make those failures visible.',
  },
  {
    n: '04',
    tone: 'primary',
    title: 'Give everything you publish for agents a purpose and a test.',
    body: 'Recipes, skills, instruction files, and servers do not help by existing. Run the evaluation with and without each one. Keep what serves its purpose. Fix or remove what harms.',
  },
  {
    n: '05',
    tone: 'amber',
    title: 'Make agent work easy to verify.',
    body: 'A person still has to check what the agent produced, and that checking can become the constraint. Test modes, dry runs, validators, and clear success signals reduce it.',
  },
  {
    n: '06',
    tone: 'purple',
    title: 'Agent-facing content is a supply chain.',
    body: 'Review changes, record where content came from, and assume someone will try to poison it.',
  },
  {
    n: '07',
    tone: 'primary',
    title: 'Do not tax the humans.',
    body: 'Agent programs must not add load to open-source maintainers or fill community spaces with generated content.',
  },
];

const toneBorder: Record<string, string> = { primary: 'border-primary', amber: 'border-amber', purple: 'border-purple' };
const toneText: Record<string, string> = { primary: '', amber: 'text-amber', purple: 'text-purple' };

const comparisonRows = [
  {
    left: 'Human community remains the primary trust mechanism',
    right: 'Agents work from what they can read and call: documentation, code, schemas, tools',
  },
  {
    left: 'Feedback arrives through forums, tickets, and conversations',
    right: 'Agent failures stay silent until instrumented: evaluations, agent traffic, a reporting channel',
  },
  {
    left: 'Documentation written for human navigation',
    right: 'Documentation also delivered in a form agents can fetch, and tested for whether it helps',
  },
  {
    left: 'SDK and API designed for developer ergonomics',
    right: 'Errors that name the remedy, sign-in that works without a browser, a safe place to test',
  },
  {
    left: 'Metrics: reach, engagement, community health',
    right: 'Metrics: selection, completion (FAISR), cost, artifact lift, agent reach, outcome link',
  },
  {
    left: 'Human community work keeps its value as generated content spreads',
    right: 'The two are linked: what people say in public may influence what agents choose',
  },
];
---

<PageLayout
  title="What Is Agentic Developer Relations?"
  description="The definition of Agentic Developer Relations — the practice within Developer Relations that represents developers who work through AI agents — with the agent journey, the seven principles, and how it is measured."
>
  <section class="container-site pt-16 pb-4">
    <p class="eyebrow mb-4">Definition</p>
    <h1 class="max-w-3xl">What is Agentic Developer Relations?</h1>
    <p class="lead text-muted mt-5 max-w-2xl">
      The definition, the agent journey, the seven principles, and how it
      is measured.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container-prose pb-16">
    <DefinitionBox term="Agentic Developer Relations (AR)">
      The practice within Developer Relations that represents developers who
      work through AI agents. It measures how well agents choose, understand,
      build on, and operate a platform; turns what it finds into changes in
      the product, the documentation, and the public record; and keeps the
      human community healthy as agent activity grows.
    </DefinitionBox>

    <p class="mt-6">
      Agentic Developer Relations does not replace Developer Relations. It
      extends it, because a growing share of developers now reach a platform
      through an AI agent: asking it which product to use, having it write
      the integration, and increasingly letting it operate the product for
      them. A person still approves the work and answers for the result.
      The practice serves that person by paying attention to their agent.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>The core insight: two audiences, one platform</h2>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Every developer platform has always had one audience: human developers.
        Documentation, SDKs, APIs, tutorials, support channels — all of it
        designed for people who read, type, ask questions, and remember.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        That has changed. When a developer asks GitHub Copilot, Cursor, or
        Claude Code to write an integration with your platform, there are now
        two parties making decisions: the developer, who evaluates the output,
        and the AI agent, who generates it. The agent doesn't navigate your
        documentation. It retrieves specific sections as context for
        generation tasks. It handles ambiguity badly, and can turn an
        imprecision in your API surface or a gap in your documentation
        into integration failures at scale.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        The developer experience of your platform is now partly determined by
        how well AI agents can work with it. DevRel has always been
        responsible for the developer experience. Agentic Developer Relations is the
        recognition that this responsibility now extends to the non-human
        member of the development partnership.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-14">Where this sits: Agent Experience</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        The idea that agents are users of a platform has a name that
        predates this site. Mathias Biilmann of Netlify introduced
        <a href="https://www.netlify.com/agent-experience/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Agent Experience (AX)</a>
        in January 2025: the experience AI agents have as users of a
        product or platform.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        AX is a property of a platform, as Developer Experience is.
        Developer Relations is one of the practices that improves Developer
        Experience. Agentic Developer Relations is the corresponding
        practice for Agent Experience: the part of DevRel that finds out
        how well agents fare on a platform and carries that back to the
        product, the documentation, and the community.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Audience</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Property of the platform</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Practice that works on it</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Humans</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Developer Experience (DX)</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Developer Relations</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Agents</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Agent Experience (AX)</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Agentic Developer Relations</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6">
        Engineering teams own much of what determines AX: API design, auth,
        error models. Agentic Developer Relations does not replace that
        work. It brings the evidence of what developers and their agents
        are trying to do, and where they fail.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>The agent journey</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        An agent's dealings with a platform run through five stages. Most
        attention so far, including earlier versions of this site, has gone
        to the second and third.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Stage</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">The question</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">1. Select</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">When a developer asks an agent for a solution, does it choose this platform, a competitor, or build its own?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">2. Understand</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Can the agent get correct, current context, cheaply?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">3. Complete</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Does it reach a working integration the developer can verify? At what cost, and with how much human help?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">4. Operate</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Can an agent act on the platform at runtime, within limits its user has set?</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">5. Report</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">When something fails, does the company find out?</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="mt-6">
        <strong>Selection</strong> comes first because a platform that is
        never chosen has no success rate to improve. In
        <a href="https://amplifying.ai/research/claude-code-picks/report" target="_blank" rel="noopener">one study of Claude Code</a>, the agent built its own
        solution in 12 of 20 product categories, and where it did pick a
        product it converged on a small default set.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        <strong>Operation</strong> is a different kind of problem, and one
        shared with engineering. An agent acting for a user needs narrowly
        scoped, delegated credentials, spending and rate limits, a way to
        revoke access, an audit trail, operations that are safe to repeat,
        and recovery from half-finished work. The aim is bounded,
        observable risk.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        <strong>Reporting</strong> needs a mechanism. Surveys and log
        analysis are indirect. A further option, untested so far, is to
        give agents a direct way to report a failure, such as a feedback
        tool or a documented endpoint, with every report treated as
        untrusted input.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-site">
      <div class="container-prose px-0">
        <h2>The seven principles</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="mt-10 grid md:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-3 gap-6">
        {
          principles.map((p) => (
            <div class={`border-t-4 ${toneBorder[p.tone]} border-x border-b border-border rounded-lg bg-surface p-6`}>
              <p class={`eyebrow mb-2 ${toneText[p.tone]}`}>Principle {p.n}</p>
              <h3 class="text-heading text-lg font-bold mb-3 leading-snug">{p.title}</h3>
              <p class="text-body text-sm leading-relaxed">{p.body}</p>
            </div>
          ))
        }
      </div>

      <div class="container-prose px-0 mt-10">
        <p>
          Principle 4 replaces an earlier one, "Encoded judgment is the
          moat." Judgment about what a correct integration looks like still
          matters. What the evidence does not support is assuming that any
          particular way of encoding it helps. In
          <a href="https://vercel.com/blog/agents-md-outperforms-skills-in-our-agent-evals" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Vercel's evaluation of Next.js tasks</a>, a documentation
          index in an AGENTS.md file lifted the pass rate from 53% to 100%,
          while a skill left at its default behavior added nothing. Each
          artifact has to be tested.
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>How it is measured</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        One number is not enough. Agentic Developer Relations uses a small
        scorecard.
      </p>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto mt-6">
        <table class="w-full border-collapse text-left">
          <thead>
            <tr class="bg-primary-surface">
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Measure</th>
              <th class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">What it answers</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Selection rate</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">How often an agent chooses the platform for a neutral request in its category, and how often it builds its own.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Completion rate</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Whether the agent reaches a working integration. Reported with no human intervention (FAISR) and with a stated number of interventions.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Cost</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Turns, tokens, and time per run, including the runs that fail.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Artifact lift</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Whether a given skill, server, file, or documentation change serves its purpose, tested with and without it.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Agent reach</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Whether agents are arriving, and whether they use what is published for them.</td>
            </tr>
            <tr class="align-top">
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm font-semibold">Outcome link</td>
              <td class="p-4 border border-border text-sm">Whether any of this moves adoption. A hypothesis, to be tested against activation and retention.</td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <Callout type="primary" label="FAISR">
        <strong>First-Attempt Integration Success Rate:</strong> the share
        of runs in which an agent, given one developer request, reaches an
        integration that passes the graders with no further human input.
        Within a run the agent may retry on its own.
      </Callout>

      <p class="mt-5">
        FAISR was this site's original headline metric. It remains the
        completion measure, alongside the others. Most DevRel programs
        cannot yet answer the FAISR question for their platform, and that
        is still the place to start.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        <a href="/measurement/" class="font-semibold">
          The full scorecard, and how to start →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-prose">
      <h2>Two constraints: the environment and review</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        A faster model cannot recover the time an agent spends on slow
        tools, human-only sign-in, and unclear errors. If 80% of an
        integration is tool time, the most any model can do for the whole
        is 1.25×. That is Amdahl's Law, and Jeff Dean made the same point
        about agent tool latency at NVIDIA GTC in March 2026
        (<a href="https://officechai.com/ai/will-need-to-re-engineer-tools-so-that-they-can-keep-up-with-the-speeds-of-ai-agents-googles-jeff-dean/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">as reported by OfficeChai</a>).
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        The second constraint is the developer's time spent checking what
        the agent produced. <a href="https://www.infoq.com/news/2026/10/jellyfish-ai-productivity/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">A Harvard study of engineering data, as reported by InfoQ</a>,
        found pull request review time up 49% where coding agents were
        adopted. A platform eases the first constraint by being fast and
        clear for agents, and the second by being easy to verify.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5">
        <a href="/the-amdahl-tax/" class="font-semibold">
          More on the two constraints, and what to build →
        </a>
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section container-site">
    <div class="container-prose px-0">
      <h2>How it extends the Fundamental Triad</h2>
      <p class="mt-5">
        The Fundamental Triad of Developer Relations — Community, Company,
        Customer — assumed every vertex was human. In the Agentic Developer Relations
        era, each vertex gains an extension into the agent ecosystem.
      </p>
    </div>
    <FundamentalTriad />
  </section>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container-site">
      <div class="container-prose px-0 mb-4">
        <h2>What changes, what stays the same</h2>
      </div>
      <CompareTable
        leftHeading="Traditional DevRel — unchanged"
        rightHeading="Agentic Developer Relations — added"
        rows={comparisonRows}
      />

      <div class="container-prose px-0 mt-8">
        <p>
          <a href="/devrel-patterns-reframed/" class="font-semibold">
            Continue to: The 37 Patterns Reframed →
          </a>
        </p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>
</PageLayout>
